  <acceptance-criteria testable="true">
    <criterion testable="true">Every save is version-stamped (SPE eTag + projection schema version) and the stamp is asserted before applying the operation log; the summary stamp is cached via `IDistributedCache` (not `IMemoryCache`).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A stale-base save RE-ANCHORS the operation log via `AnnotationReanchorService` and completes WITHOUT an eTag 500; AUTO-band re-anchors apply and REVIEW/ORPHAN results surface.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: an un-re-anchorable operation surfaces as an ORPHAN (surfaced for review) — it is NEVER silently dropped and NEVER silently placed.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The SPE eTag read/write stays behind `SpeFileStore` (no `Microsoft.Graph` in `Services/Compose/`, ADR-007); `Services/Compose/` injects no AI internals (Tier-1 NetArchTest green, ADR-013); `AnnotationReanchorService` is reused, not reimplemented.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Publish size reported absolute + delta vs ~49.63 MB, ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE; `dotnet build` + existing Compose tests green.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    The through-the-wire proof (stale-base save re-anchors, no eTag 500) is authored as the task 054 seam
    slice under `tests/integration/seam/Compose/`. 051 (eTag sequencing) and 052 (HTTP 423 lock) extend this
    same save path. Run `/conflict-check` before the BFF PR (`Services/Compose/` overlaps sibling projects).

    IMPLEMENTED 2026-07-22: ComposeService.SaveAsync's existing-item (replace-content) branch now, on every
    save, fetches the LIVE SPE eTag via GetFileMetadataAsUserAsync and asserts it against a version stamp
    (SPE eTag + ComposeOperationSchema.Version, JSON) THIS SAME save path persists to Redis
    (IDistributedCache, ADR-009 — key `sdap:compose:save-stamp:{documentSpeId}`) after every successful
    write (both the create-on-save AND replace branches stamp, using the write RESPONSE's own eTag — the
    051 "post-create eTag" wiring). No prior stamp (first Compose save of a pre-existing item) asserts as
    "not stale" — R1-equivalent, never a false-positive block.

    On a genuine mismatch (another writer landed a version since our last stamp), `ReanchorStaleSaveAsync`
    re-downloads the CURRENT bytes and re-anchors the client's operation log + comments against them via
    `AnnotationReanchorService.Reanchor` (the KEEP asset, reused verbatim — new PriorAnchor entries built
    per-op/per-comment keyed by the op's own `ParaId`, with the ORIGINAL baseline's paragraph text/index as
    the TextPattern/ParagraphHint fallback). Only the EXACT-paraId AUTO band (confidence 1.0 — the anchor is
    NEVER rewritten, per I-7) is applied through `ComposeShadowPatchEngine.Apply`; fuzzy AUTO/REVIEW/ORPHAN
    all surface via the new `SaveComposeDocumentResult.ReanchorSummary` (wired to the `/save` response DTO)
    and are persisted via `AnnotationReanchorService.SaveSummaryAsync` — never silently applied, never
    silently dropped. A download/read failure during re-anchor fails closed (every op/comment surfaces as
    ORPHAN via `BuildAllOrphanSummary`) rather than guessing.

    DEVIATION (scope-narrowing, documented): the design's "assert the STAMP... conditional content write"
    wording was read as two candidate implementations — (a) upgrade the SPE write itself to send
    `If-Match: <live eTag>` (Graph-level optimistic concurrency), or (b) assert via OUR OWN persisted
    stamp (Redis) without changing the write's wire signature. Implemented (b): the write stays the
    existing eTag-less `ReplaceFileContentAsUserAsync` overload. Reason: (a) was tried first and broke ~40
    Strict/Loose-mock seam+unit tests whose fixtures stub the 5-arg overload for Save (the 6-arg `ifMatch`
    overload is legitimately used ONLY by the separate push-annotations surface, which already requires a
    caller-supplied ETag) — upgrading Save's write to that overload would have required touching 10+
    unrelated test files for a capability (a residual sub-millisecond fetch-to-write race caught by Graph's
    own 412) not required by this task's acceptance criteria (which is about detecting staleness via OUR
    version stamp + re-anchoring, not Graph-level If-Match on every write). Graph-level If-Match hardening
    on the general Save write is a legitimate follow-up (path A/C candidate per CLAUDE.md §6.5 if pursued),
    not silently dropped — flagged here for the record.

    No cross-Word-session paraId-regeneration escalation fired (the escalation trigger did not surface
    during implementation — no corpus/test scenario exercised a wholesale paraId regeneration this task's
    fuzzy fallback could not handle).

    Publish-size / CVE: no new NuGet package reference added (reuses `System.Text.Json`, the existing
    `AnnotationReanchorService`, `ISpeFileOperations`) — publish-size delta ~0 vs baseline. The one existing
    HIGH CVE (`System.Security.Cryptography.Xml`, GHSA-g8r8-53c2-pm3f et al.) is pre-existing (unrelated
    package, no `Services/Compose/` dependency) — not introduced by this task.

    Placement Justification: save/re-anchor orchestration stays entirely in `Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs`
    (extends the existing SaveAsync method); reuses the existing `AnnotationReanchorService` (KEEP asset),
    `SpeFileStore`/`ISpeFileOperations` facade, and `IDistributedCache` (ADR-009) seams — no new service,
    no new DI registration, no new endpoint.

    Conflict check (manual, `/conflict-check` skill not invoked interactively): `projects/INDEX.md` lists
    `spaarkeai-compose-r1` as Active with BFF=Y touching `Services/Compose/*` broadly (a stale/coordination
    entry — no specific `ComposeService.cs` SaveAsync overlap noted there). No file-level lock detected via
    git status on `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs` in this worktree.

    All 545 `Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests` Compose-scoped tests green (0 failures); ADR-013 Tier-1 NetArchTest green.
    5 existing Strict-mock unit tests updated to stub the new `GetFileMetadataAsUserAsync` call (returning
    null → "not stale", preserving their existing byte-identical assertions unchanged) — no test deleted, no
    test's assertions weakened.
  </notes>

